{
  "text": "Welcome to the Huberman Lab Podcast. Today we discuss dopamine, motivation, and the neural circuits of drive. Whenever you experience a peak in dopamine, your baseline drops below where it was before. And this is the dopamine baseline fallacy. If you artificially spike your dopamine with stimulants or rapid rewards, your brain resets downward. You feel an intense crash right after the peak wears off. The wider the peak, the deeper the trough that follows. This is why chronic pursuit of dopamine inevitably produces apathy. To maintain motivation, you must attach the reward to the effort itself. If you celebrate only the win, you guarantee friction on the next endeavor. When you celebrate the outcome, you train your brain to hate the friction required to get there. Instead, tell yourself that the friction is where neuroplasticity happens. Evoking friction as the reward resets your baseline higher over time. This protocol creates an internal buffer against burnout. Your dopamine stays stable throughout the entire week.",
